And they are very helpful like I just

00:36:11.665 --> 00:36:16.330
found this unknowns community colleges down south and I start taking courses.

00:36:16.330 --> 00:36:21.475
I provided the coursework to this PD and they said, "Yes, that's fine."

00:36:21.475 --> 00:36:23.560
And it was just, you know, same day.

00:36:23.560 --> 00:36:25.390
It's like, "Okay, you can take that we'll accept it."

00:36:25.390 --> 00:36:27.460
And then they accepted everything,

00:36:27.460 --> 00:36:31.735
and I was able to meet all the prerequisite to take some of the courses.

00:36:31.735 --> 00:36:33.955
And as far as the technologies,

00:36:33.955 --> 00:36:35.830
I think that's where also where

00:36:35.830 --> 00:36:39.745
the Interacting with TAs and students is important because,

00:36:39.745 --> 00:36:41.800
where like at first they're like,

00:36:41.800 --> 00:36:43.210
they're drawing these, um,

00:36:43.210 --> 00:36:47.020
okay this is on Canvas and this is on Piazza so it was Canvas and Piazza.

00:36:47.020 --> 00:36:49.870
But it's that you- you got to interact with people to

00:36:49.870 --> 00:36:53.275
find out where the Canvas- what is Canvas and how to use it.

00:36:53.275 --> 00:36:57.025
And what's Piazza and wha- how to use Piazza, you know.

00:36:57.025 --> 00:36:58.660
Because there's no manual to use it,

00:36:58.660 --> 00:37:00.415
you just have to learn it on your own and,

00:37:00.415 --> 00:37:03.265
and everybody's helpful you just have to ask the question.

00:37:03.265 --> 00:37:05.215
Yeah, yeah. And I would say,

00:37:05.215 --> 00:37:06.610
you know, to echo I mean,

00:37:06.610 --> 00:37:10.600
I guess this makes me look bias but I would say we have a good response time to email.

00:37:10.600 --> 00:37:12.220
[OVERLAPPING] So, if you're having any confusion

00:37:12.220 --> 00:37:14.560
about our application, our enrollment system,

00:37:14.560 --> 00:37:19.060
or you know, once you're in a course where- what is Canvas? What is Piazza?

00:37:19.060 --> 00:37:20.560
How do I get my Stanford email, you know,

00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:23.470
those are all things that our team is here to do.

00:37:23.470 --> 00:37:25.720
Um, I answer a lot of those emails every day.

00:37:25.720 --> 00:37:29.260
So, we're definitely trying to make it as easy a process as possible,

00:37:29.260 --> 00:37:31.480
especially because you are remote you can't just

00:37:31.480 --> 00:37:34.120
show up to your Adviser or to your Professor and say,

00:37:34.120 --> 00:37:35.920
I don't understand what's going on.

00:37:35.920 --> 00:37:39.115
Um, so we try to be really mindful of that in our response times.

00:37:39.115 --> 00:37:42.790
And, uh, just to also reiterate about the application process.

00:37:42.790 --> 00:37:44.590
We try to keep it as simple as possible

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:49.060
yes these are Stanford courses but unlike applying to a master's program, you know,

00:37:49.060 --> 00:37:51.790
if you're qualified to take the course and you've- you've enrolled

00:37:51.790 --> 00:37:54.655
on time and sent the application and the transcripts.

00:37:54.655 --> 00:37:56.170
You'll most likely be approved.

00:37:56.170 --> 00:37:58.720
The departments just wanna make sure qualified people are in there,

00:37:58.720 --> 00:38:02.545
but they're not like we only admit 30 percent, you know.

00:38:02.545 --> 00:38:06.250
Um, and, and we try to get you answers as fast as possible,

00:38:06.250 --> 00:38:09.205
it usually takes a few weeks from when you apply and enroll.

00:38:09.205 --> 00:38:11.890
And, and I'll mention this again at the end of

00:38:11.890 --> 00:38:15.925
the presentation by winter quarter enrollment is open so if you know,

00:38:15.925 --> 00:38:17.515
this presentation sold you,

00:38:17.515 --> 00:38:21.190
you can just get right into it and apply and probably enroll by the end of this week,

00:38:21.190 --> 00:38:22.405
that's how easy it is.

00:38:22.405 --> 00:38:24.580
So, um, yeah definitely I'd echo that.

00:38:24.580 --> 00:38:26.605
We try to keep it as simple as possible and,

00:38:26.605 --> 00:38:30.640
and also improve our communication around all the different platforms and systems,

00:38:30.640 --> 00:38:34.240
because that is something we recognize can be a little confusing for

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:38.125
a new student or someone that hasn't been in college for a while.

00:38:38.125 --> 00:38:41.530
Um, so we got a new question that is

00:38:41.530 --> 00:38:45.355
asking how you decided to take courses here at Stanford,

00:38:45.355 --> 00:38:51.205
rather than another online platform like Coursera or Udacity or things like that.

00:38:51.205 --> 00:38:54.790
Yeah, I think that's a- that's a very good question.

00:38:54.790 --> 00:39:00.175
Um, I mean, I did take online courses like Udacity or Coursera.

00:39:00.175 --> 00:39:03.760
But I think personally, what I've felt with online courses is,

00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:07.630
um, I personally cannot find myself to commit to them.

00:39:07.630 --> 00:39:10.960
Um, maybe it's a force function, maybe it's Stanford,

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:12.325
maybe it's the, you know,

00:39:12.325 --> 00:39:14.320
the- the association of folks around here,

00:39:14.320 --> 00:39:15.970
maybe it's the group study.

00:39:15.970 --> 00:39:19.180
Um, I personally I am not a big fan of,

00:39:19.180 --> 00:39:20.980
you know, what do you call them,

00:39:20.980 --> 00:39:22.390
there's a nice acronym.

00:39:22.390 --> 00:39:23.665
Self-paced? MOOCS?

00:39:23.665 --> 00:39:24.340
MOOCS! Yeah.

00:39:24.340 --> 00:39:26.290
Yeah. So, yeah, I mean,

00:39:26.290 --> 00:39:27.550
uh, I mean maybe it's, uh,

00:39:27.550 --> 00:39:29.155
maybe it's a personal thing but, uh,

00:39:29.155 --> 00:39:31.000
for me to succeed,

00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:32.890
uh, and be focused on, you know,

00:39:32.890 --> 00:39:35.020
provide, you know, my best I think I need to

00:39:35.020 --> 00:39:37.525
be involved with a group of people who I can see,

00:39:37.525 --> 00:39:42.160
you know, and- and know an- and I think some to provides you kinda like all of that.

00:39:42.160 --> 00:39:44.470
But, uh, but like,

00:39:44.470 --> 00:39:47.050
you know, the flexibility of doing it when you want to do it,

00:39:47.050 --> 00:39:48.505
which is- which is, I mean,

00:39:48.505 --> 00:39:51.760
pretty- which is very hard to find in- in- in online courses.

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:53.350
So, um, yeah.

00:39:53.350 --> 00:39:55.315
Also the- just-just- you know,

00:39:55.315 --> 00:39:58.405
the- I think the best part I like about this course is,

00:39:58.405 --> 00:40:03.040
your schedule is aligned with the- with the full time students, so that way,

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:05.440
you know, you have- you're now accountable to not just,

00:40:05.440 --> 00:40:07.750
you know, your- yourself but to the group that you're working with.

00:40:07.750 --> 00:40:09.520
So, it's- it's time-bounded and, you know,

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:12.040
for some of the online courses even though they are time bounded,

00:40:12.040 --> 00:40:13.555
just doesn't work the same way.

00:40:13.555 --> 00:40:14.140
Yeah.

00:40:14.140 --> 00:40:14.965
Yeah.

00:40:14.965 --> 00:40:16.735
What about you?

00:40:16.735 --> 00:40:22.720
For me it's like whether you take a course at Stanford or MOOC or Coursera, take- yet,

00:40:22.720 --> 00:40:25.390
it still commit your time to do it and I

00:40:25.390 --> 00:40:28.630
guess you have to think about what's more meaningful to you.

00:40:28.630 --> 00:40:31.660
And the- just by looking at the people

00:40:31.660 --> 00:40:35.094
the Stanford program attracts and then the faculty's they offer,

00:40:35.094 --> 00:40:38.320
like where do you go and learn about US-Russia relationship,

00:40:38.320 --> 00:40:41.950
uh, from a former ambassador of- to Russia, right?

00:40:41.950 --> 00:40:44.590
[LAUGHTER] So, and then is- that list goes on and on.

00:40:44.590 --> 00:40:44.690
Right.

00:40:44.690 --> 00:40:46.885
So, um, so then,

00:40:46.885 --> 00:40:49.990
you have to think about if you're going to commit your time, you know,

00:40:49.990 --> 00:40:51.775
what is more meaningful to you, like,

00:40:51.775 --> 00:40:55.420
or what was more worth to you, you know, so.

00:40:55.420 --> 00:40:58.870
Yeah, awesome. Um, I think were kind

00:40:58.870 --> 00:41:01.840
of [inaudible] now starting to get into repetitive questions around balancing work,

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:03.520
but is there anything else that you'd like to share about

00:41:03.520 --> 00:41:06.040
your experiences as a remote student?

00:41:06.040 --> 00:41:08.950
[LAUGHTER].

00:41:08.950 --> 00:41:11.620
Oh, I guess in terms of balancing work,

00:41:11.620 --> 00:41:13.720
I think that's always gonna be a challenge.

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:16.090
I mean, it's- there's always gonna be different challenges

00:41:16.090 --> 00:41:18.654
at different points in time because these courses,

00:41:18.654 --> 00:41:20.620
you know, span over years, right?

00:41:20.620 --> 00:41:24.445
I mean, if you're really serious about doing like a- like a masters program, you know,

00:41:24.445 --> 00:41:26.770
and you're talking about a couple of years, right,

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:30.430
before you- so you think you- you'll be in different points in life at that point.

00:41:30.430 --> 00:41:31.690
So, I think, uh,

00:41:31.690 --> 00:41:33.070
keeping your, I guess,

00:41:33.070 --> 00:41:35.650
your goal centered to what you want and then you're looking at

00:41:35.650 --> 00:41:38.320
life around that is- is- is what I do.

00:41:38.320 --> 00:41:40.300
And, you know, it's- it's definitely hard, I mean,

00:41:40.300 --> 00:41:42.400
I won't advertise anything here,

00:41:42.400 --> 00:41:44.650
I mean, it takes a lot of commitment and sacrifice.

00:41:44.650 --> 00:41:46.060
But I think at the end of the day,

00:41:46.060 --> 00:41:47.305
if- if that's what you want,

00:41:47.305 --> 00:41:49.225
you know, that's where your passion then you make it work.

00:41:49.225 --> 00:41:49.660
Yeah.

00:41:49.660 --> 00:41:50.680
Okay.

00:41:50.680 --> 00:41:51.250
Dido. [LAUGHTER].

00:41:51.250 --> 00:41:55.120
Dido? [LAUGHTER] Um, I actually have another question for you, guys.

00:41:55.120 --> 00:41:59.020
Did you- I don't remember your program plans off the top of my head, um,

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:01.540
but did you guys take courses every quarter since

00:42:01.540 --> 00:42:04.735
you've started or have you taken breaks or what does that look like?

00:42:04.735 --> 00:42:06.895
I- I- I have taken,

00:42:06.895 --> 00:42:08.110
yeah, I've taken a break.

00:42:08.110 --> 00:42:10.135
So, I've taken courses, um,

00:42:10.135 --> 00:42:13.330
for the first, um- so what I did was for the first two quarters,

00:42:13.330 --> 00:42:15.310
I took it back to back and then, you know,

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:17.950
I had to switch my job so I took a break and then,

00:42:17.950 --> 00:42:19.150
you know, arriving in my course.

00:42:19.150 --> 00:42:21.700
So, yeah, taking a break.

00:42:21.700 --> 00:42:23.245
Yeah. How about you?

00:42:23.245 --> 00:42:26.545
I- I took break to meet-

00:42:26.545 --> 00:42:30.265
take the classical prerequisite in the middle because in the beginning,

00:42:30.265 --> 00:42:32.950
the courses do not require prerequisite,

00:42:32.950 --> 00:42:35.935
um, to statistics and then into international security.

00:42:35.935 --> 00:42:38.620
And then, I take a break about a year to take

00:42:38.620 --> 00:42:42.130
all the calculus requirement and then computer programming requirement.

00:42:42.130 --> 00:42:45.340
[NOISE] And I took MSE courses.

00:42:45.340 --> 00:42:48.790
So, I've been taking maybe one to two classes a quarter

00:42:48.790 --> 00:42:53.335
depending on the workload and there are resources available to find,

00:42:53.335 --> 00:42:56.680
um, how hard or difficult of courses.

00:42:56.680 --> 00:42:58.345
Uh, there's a Stanford student,

00:42:58.345 --> 00:43:00.925
very smart guy created this website.

00:43:00.925 --> 00:43:01.315
Carta?

00:43:01.315 --> 00:43:01.735
Carta.

00:43:01.735 --> 00:43:02.050
Yeah.

00:43:02.050 --> 00:43:04.180
And that you can go there and then you can get

00:43:04.180 --> 00:43:06.910
all the insights about the course and average grade.

00:43:06.910 --> 00:43:10.150
And every time each students spend for

00:43:10.150 --> 00:43:14.065
the course and- and you can kind of plan that, uh, according to the source.

00:43:14.065 --> 00:43:18.355
Using that sometime I decide to take another coursework looking,

00:43:18.355 --> 00:43:21.790
"Oh, this one takes one hour a week and this one takes one hour a week."

00:43:21.790 --> 00:43:24.460
And like probably combined that in one quarter.

00:43:24.460 --> 00:43:26.185
And another course it says,

00:43:26.185 --> 00:43:29.020
three hours a week or four hours a week and I'll only take one.

00:43:29.020 --> 00:43:33.625
So, um, there are ways that you can kinda plan it.

00:43:33.625 --> 00:43:33.925
Yeah.

00:43:33.925 --> 00:43:35.140
And there were resources available.

00:43:35.140 --> 00:43:38.335
And again, just talking to other students and they- they will help you,

00:43:38.335 --> 00:43:40.270
so take this course and take that course.

00:43:40.270 --> 00:43:42.100
Yeah. And I can speak to that too.

00:43:42.100 --> 00:43:45.370
We, um, employ a few current graduate students and some of

00:43:45.370 --> 00:43:48.730
the higher enrollment departments like Management Science and Engineering,

00:43:48.730 --> 00:43:52.105
Computer Science, Statistics and Electrical Engineering.

00:43:52.105 --> 00:43:54.790
And we have course adviser emails on our website,

00:43:54.790 --> 00:43:56.065
and you can email, you know,

00:43:56.065 --> 00:43:57.880
EE course adviser directly and say, "Hey,

00:43:57.880 --> 00:44:01.300
like I'm interested in this certificate or this specific course.

00:44:01.300 --> 00:44:02.530
What is the workload look like,

00:44:02.530 --> 00:44:04.990
what's a logical order to take the courses,

00:44:04.990 --> 00:44:07.180
um, or this is my background what do you think? "

00:44:07.180 --> 00:44:09.610
And they are so knowledgeable about this so they can give you

00:44:09.610 --> 00:44:12.010
really really detailed- way better answers

00:44:12.010 --> 00:44:14.650
than I could ever give about whether or not this makes sense for you.

00:44:14.650 --> 00:44:18.370
Um, so that's definitely something that I recommend people utilize,

00:44:18.370 --> 00:44:21.085
maybe before deciding on of course to enroll or, you know,

00:44:21.085 --> 00:44:25.000
reach out to us and we'll- we'll loop them in if we think it makes sense.

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:28.750
Um, and then also just to reiterate what you guys have already touched on,

00:44:28.750 --> 00:44:30.685
you don't have to take courses every quarter.

00:44:30.685 --> 00:44:32.530
If you're pursuing a graduate certificate,

00:44:32.530 --> 00:44:34.330
we give you three years from when you

00:44:34.330 --> 00:44:36.580
finished your first course to finish your certificate.

00:44:36.580 --> 00:44:38.260
So, it's a lot of time, usually,

00:44:38.260 --> 00:44:39.910
certificates only require four courses,

00:44:39.910 --> 00:44:43.000
so you can really take your time and take quarters off.

00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:46.165
Um, and- and, you know, like you said,

00:44:46.165 --> 00:44:49.540
use different resources, I don't think I'm allowed to sanction Carta, it's like,

00:44:49.540 --> 00:44:52.075
you should check how many hours your [LAUGHTER] classes but,

00:44:52.075 --> 00:44:54.400
I do know a lot of students use it, um,

00:44:54.400 --> 00:44:57.670
and that can be a really helpful way to just plan around what's going on.

00:44:57.670 --> 00:44:58.720
Also, if you, you know,

00:44:58.720 --> 00:45:02.320
if you are in communication with your managers and- and maybe you guys

00:45:02.320 --> 00:45:06.070
can speak to this more of your experience working with your manager through this.

00:45:06.070 --> 00:45:08.965
You know, I talked to a lot of students where they,

00:45:08.965 --> 00:45:11.320
you know, said, "Well, work project just randomly came up."

00:45:11.320 --> 00:45:13.840
And- and sometimes that's unavoidable and you have to drop a class,

00:45:13.840 --> 00:45:15.670
and- and we're there to help you through it,

00:45:15.670 --> 00:45:20.710
but as much as you can communicate with your managers at your work and kind of know well.

00:45:20.710 --> 00:45:22.165
Spring has a big project,

00:45:22.165 --> 00:45:23.800
I won't take a course then.

00:45:23.800 --> 00:45:25.855
Summer's going to be pretty quiet,

00:45:25.855 --> 00:45:27.730
that's a great time for me to do something.

00:45:27.730 --> 00:45:30.730
I think that's a great strategy to employ as well and I hope

00:45:30.730 --> 00:45:33.790
you guys have specific examples of working with your manager,

00:45:33.790 --> 00:45:36.640
whoever and it kind of has support through this program?

00:45:36.640 --> 00:45:38.560
Yeah. No. I- I think I can talk to that.

00:45:38.560 --> 00:45:40.405
So, like, for example, you know,

00:45:40.405 --> 00:45:42.280
one of the things you have to plan is like some of

00:45:42.280 --> 00:45:44.845
these courses are only offering a certain quarter.

00:45:44.845 --> 00:45:45.250
Yes.

00:45:45.250 --> 00:45:47.515
So, if you miss it in the last quarter and you know,

00:45:47.515 --> 00:45:49.630
you miss it again then you just have one more year to do it.

00:45:49.630 --> 00:45:52.270
So, that a little bit of planning needs to be done on your end.

00:45:52.270 --> 00:45:53.560
But I think, um,

00:45:53.560 --> 00:45:54.805
for me especially, you know,

00:45:54.805 --> 00:45:56.500
during the first course, you know,

00:45:56.500 --> 00:45:58.810
I specifically went up to my manager and said to him,

00:45:58.810 --> 00:46:00.220
"I need your support here for this quarter.

00:46:00.220 --> 00:46:01.930
I'm doing this, it's pretty intense course and,

00:46:01.930 --> 00:46:03.430
you know, I may, you know,

00:46:03.430 --> 00:46:05.065
requires some flexible work hours.

00:46:05.065 --> 00:46:06.940
It's not that I'm not gonna do what you want me do.

00:46:06.940 --> 00:46:08.770
I'm going to probably do more but, you know,

00:46:08.770 --> 00:46:11.605
it just for need a support and, you know, helping me.

00:46:11.605 --> 00:46:13.030
Maybe if I want to- you know,

00:46:13.030 --> 00:46:15.280
maybe go out in an office hours during the workday."

00:46:15.280 --> 00:46:17.275
And I think generally people are,

00:46:17.275 --> 00:46:18.490
you know, I mean,

00:46:18.490 --> 00:46:20.350
if your manager is a good manager, I mean,

00:46:20.350 --> 00:46:21.850
they're- they're fine with it then, you know,

00:46:21.850 --> 00:46:23.815
you can- you can walk around that.

00:46:23.815 --> 00:46:25.870
But, yeah, I mean that's-

00:46:25.870 --> 00:46:28.870
Yeah. Doing that as well?

00:46:28.870 --> 00:46:34.030
I will because I- I was intentionally taking classes that's- were unrelated

00:46:34.030 --> 00:46:38.857
to my work because- and so I was on my own. [LAUGHTER]

00:46:38.857 --> 00:46:41.000
But you can still make it work.

00:46:41.000 --> 00:46:41.315
Yeah.

00:46:41.315 --> 00:46:44.220
Again, beca- because, I was doing that

00:46:44.220 --> 00:46:47.525
because I was actually taking courses that I want to take.

00:46:47.525 --> 00:46:48.940
I want something I wanna learn about.

00:46:48.940 --> 00:46:51.885
So, you know, I actually wanted to make time,

00:46:51.885 --> 00:46:54.615
commitment to make it work so.

00:46:54.615 --> 00:46:58.440
Yeah, and, um, I'll just speak actually on a personal level.

00:46:58.440 --> 00:47:00.549
I have not taken any courses through SCPD,

00:47:00.549 --> 00:47:01.680
[LAUGHTER], I just work here [OVERLAPPING].

00:47:01.680 --> 00:47:05.470
But I actually just started a part-time master's program myself,

00:47:05.470 --> 00:47:08.650
um, and it's kinda on the weekends where I have to go to the class.

00:47:08.650 --> 00:47:10.160
But even just doing that,

00:47:10.160 --> 00:47:14.700
I've found that being honest with my team and with my manager and saying, "Hey.

00:47:14.700 --> 00:47:17.760
This is what I'm doing, there's gonna be a day when I need to leave early to get to

00:47:17.760 --> 00:47:21.305
class or just have a big thing due this week like."

00:47:21.305 --> 00:47:24.345
You know and also setting that expectation for myself with school,

00:47:24.345 --> 00:47:28.360
like I know there are certain weeks when enrollment is my main priority for this job,

